    "thm-finite-description-compression-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-probability-measure-basic-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space, let $A,B\\in\\mathcal F$,\nand let $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be events.\n\n1. $\\mathbb P(A^c)=1-\\mathbb P(A)$.\n2. If $A\\subseteq B$, then $\\mathbb P(A)\\le\\mathbb P(B)$.\n3. If $A\\subseteq B$, then $\\mathbb P(B\\setminus A)=\\mathbb P(B)-\\mathbb P(A)$.\n4. $\\mathbb P(\\bigcup_n A_n)\\le\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\mathbb P(A_n)$, and for every\n   natural $m$,\n   $$\\mathbb P\\left(\\bigcup_{n<m}A_n\\right)\\le\\sum_{n<m}\\mathbb P(A_n).$$\n5. $\\mathbb P(A\\cup B)=\\mathbb P(A)+\\mathbb P(B)-\\mathbb P(A\\cap B)$.\n6. If $A_n\\uparrow A$, then\n   $$\\mathbb P(A)=\\sup_n\\mathbb P(A_n).$$\n   If $A_n\\downarrow A$, then\n   $$\\mathbb P(A)=\\inf_n\\mathbb P(A_n).$$",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-margin-preserving-compression",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)$ be a nonempty measurable instance space, let $\\mathcal Y=\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$ have its discrete sigma-algebra with $K\\ge2$, and equip $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$ and its finite powers with their product sigma-algebras. Let $P$ be a probability law on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$. For an integer $m\\ge1$, write a sample as $S=((X_1,Y_1),\\ldots,(X_m,Y_m))$. A score map $u=(u_1,\\ldots,u_K):\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ is **coordinatewise measurable** when each $u_k:(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)\\to(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ is measurable. Define its classifier by the least-index tie rule\n\n$$h_u(x):=\\min\\{k\\in\\mathcal Y:u_k(x)=\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal Y}u_j(x)\\}$$\n\nand its margin on label $y$ by\n\n$$M_u(x,y):=u_y(x)-\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal Y,\\ j\\ne y}u_j(x).$$\n\nFor each $k\\in\\mathcal Y$,\n$$\\{x:h_u(x)=k\\}=\\bigcap_{j<k}\\{x:u_k(x)>u_j(x)\\}\\cap\\bigcap_{j>k}\\{x:u_k(x)\\ge u_j(x)\\},$$\nand each comparison set is measurable; an intersection over no indices means $\\mathcal X$. Since $\\mathcal Y$ is finite, $M_u$ is measurable on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$ as well. The population zero-one risk is\n$$R_P(h_u):=P\\{(x,y):h_u(x)\\ne y\\}.$$\nFor a sample $S$ of length $m$ and $\\gamma\\ge0$, the empirical margin loss is\n$$\\widehat L_\\gamma(u;S):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbf1\\{M_u(X_i,Y_i)\\le\\gamma\\}.$$\nThe event defining $R_P(h_u)$ is measurable by measurability of $h_u$.\n\nFix integers $q\\ge0$ and $r\\ge1$. A fixed-helper finite parameter codebook consists of a helper string $s$ fixed independently of the sample, finite nonempty sets $D_1,\\ldots,D_q$ with $|D_j|\\le r$, a nonempty code set $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\prod_{j=1}^qD_j$, and one coordinatewise measurable decoder score map $g_{s,a}:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ for each $a\\in\\mathcal A$. For $q=0$, the empty product is the singleton containing the empty tuple. The codebook and all its decoders are fixed independently of the sample, so $|\\mathcal A|\\le r^q$.\n\nLet $F:(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y)^m\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ be measurable for the product sigma-algebra on its domain and the Borel sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^K$. Let $\\alpha:(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y)^m\\to\\mathcal A$ be measurable, where $\\mathcal A$ has its discrete sigma-algebra, and write $f_S(x):=F(S,x)$. For $\\epsilon>0$, the sample-dependent trained score map $f_S$ is $\\epsilon$-compressible by this codebook on a sample $S$ if\n$$\\max_{k\\in\\mathcal Y}|f_S(X_i)_k-g_{s,\\alpha(S)}(X_i)_k|\\le\\epsilon\\qquad(1\\le i\\le m).$$\nThe code $\\alpha(S)$ may depend on $S$; the helper string, codebook, and decoders remain fixed independently of the sample.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-margin-preserving-compression",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)$ be a nonempty measurable instance space, let $\\mathcal Y=\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$ have its discrete sigma-algebra with $K\\ge2$, and equip $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$ and its finite powers with their product sigma-algebras. Let $P$ be a probability law on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$. For an integer $m\\ge1$, write a sample as $S=((X_1,Y_1),\\ldots,(X_m,Y_m))$. A score map $u=(u_1,\\ldots,u_K):\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ is **coordinatewise measurable** when each $u_k:(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)\\to(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ is measurable. Define its classifier by the least-index tie rule\n\n$$h_u(x):=\\min\\{k\\in\\mathcal Y:u_k(x)=\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal Y}u_j(x)\\}$$\n\nand its margin on label $y$ by\n\n$$M_u(x,y):=u_y(x)-\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal Y,\\ j\\ne y}u_j(x).$$\n\nFor each $k\\in\\mathcal Y$,\n$$\\{x:h_u(x)=k\\}=\\bigcap_{j<k}\\{x:u_k(x)>u_j(x)\\}\\cap\\bigcap_{j>k}\\{x:u_k(x)\\ge u_j(x)\\},$$\nand each comparison set is measurable; an intersection over no indices means $\\mathcal X$. Since $\\mathcal Y$ is finite, $M_u$ is measurable on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$ as well. The population zero-one risk is\n$$R_P(h_u):=P\\{(x,y):h_u(x)\\ne y\\}.$$\nFor a sample $S$ of length $m$ and $\\gamma\\ge0$, the empirical margin loss is\n$$\\widehat L_\\gamma(u;S):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbf1\\{M_u(X_i,Y_i)\\le\\gamma\\}.$$\nThe event defining $R_P(h_u)$ is measurable by measurability of $h_u$.\n\nFix integers $q\\ge0$ and $r\\ge1$. A fixed-helper finite parameter codebook consists of a helper string $s$ fixed independently of the sample, finite nonempty sets $D_1,\\ldots,D_q$ with $|D_j|\\le r$, a nonempty code set $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\prod_{j=1}^qD_j$, and one coordinatewise measurable decoder score map $g_{s,a}:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ for each $a\\in\\mathcal A$. For $q=0$, the empty product is the singleton containing the empty tuple. The codebook and all its decoders are fixed independently of the sample, so $|\\mathcal A|\\le r^q$.\n\nLet $F:(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y)^m\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ be measurable for the product sigma-algebra on its domain and the Borel sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^K$. Let $\\alpha:(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y)^m\\to\\mathcal A$ be measurable, where $\\mathcal A$ has its discrete sigma-algebra, and write $f_S(x):=F(S,x)$. For $\\epsilon>0$, the sample-dependent trained score map $f_S$ is $\\epsilon$-compressible by this codebook on a sample $S$ if\n$$\\max_{k\\in\\mathcal Y}|f_S(X_i)_k-g_{s,\\alpha(S)}(X_i)_k|\\le\\epsilon\\qquad(1\\le i\\le m).$$\nThe code $\\alpha(S)$ may depend on $S$; the helper string, codebook, and decoders remain fixed independently of the sample.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\exp(x)>0$ and\n$$\\exp(-x)=\\frac1{\\exp(x)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Their\n**product sigma-algebra** is\n\n$$\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B := \\sigma(\\{A \\times B : A \\in \\mathcal A,\\ B \\in \\mathcal B\\}),$$\n\nthe sigma-algebra on $X \\times Y$ generated by the measurable rectangles of\n[[def-measurable-rectangle]].\n\nFor the empty family, put $\\prod_{i<0}X_i:=\\{()\\}$ and define its **empty\nproduct sigma-algebra** by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<0}\\mathcal A_i:=\\{\\varnothing,\\{()\\}\\}.$$\n\nFor a nonempty finite family of measurable spaces $(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ with\n$i<n$, the **finite product sigma-algebra** is defined recursively by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<1}\\mathcal A_i := \\mathcal A_0,\\qquad \\bigotimes_{i<n+1}\\mathcal A_i := \\left(\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathcal A_i\\right)\\otimes\\mathcal A_n.$$\n\nThis recursive definition is the finite base case used later when the library\npasses to countable product constructions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$ and $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$ be measurable spaces\n([[def-measurable-space]]). A function $f : X \\to Y$ is **measurable** when\n\n$$f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{A} \\qquad \\text{for every } B \\in \\mathcal{B}.$$\n\nEquivalently, the family\n\n$$\\mathcal{C}_f := \\{\\, B \\subseteq Y : f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{A} \\,\\}$$\n\nis a sigma-algebra on $Y$ containing $\\mathcal{B}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space and let\n$f,g:X\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra on\nthe extended real line. Products of functions use\n$\\odot_X$ from [[def-measurable-pointwise-extended-real-product]], and $cf$\nabbreviates $\\underline c\\odot_X f$ for the constant function $\\underline c$.\nThe magnitude function is $|f|=\\max(f,-f)$ as in\n[[def-positive-and-negative-parts-of-a-function]]. Then:\n\n1. $cf$ is measurable for every real scalar $c$;\n2. $\\max(f,g)$, $\\min(f,g)$, $|f|$, $f^+$, and $f^-$ are measurable;\n3. if the partial pointwise sum $f+g$ is defined at every point, then it is measurable;\n4. the pointwise product $fg:=f\\odot_X g$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-bounded-variable-hoeffding-mgf",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Y$ be a measurable real random variable on a probability space with $0\\le Y\\le1$ almost surely. Then, for every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$\\mathbb E\\exp\\{\\lambda(Y-\\mathbb EY)\\}\\le \\exp(\\lambda^2/8).$$\n\nThis proof uses only finite product and scalar integral arguments; it does not assume the Axiom of Choice.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of random elements\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$. For each $i$, let\n$g_i:(S_i,\\Sigma_i)\\to(T_i,\\mathcal T_i)$ be measurable. Then the family\n$(g_i\\circ X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is independent.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-factorization-of-expectations-for-independent-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}$ be independent real random variables on a\ncommon probability space, and let $g_i:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be Borel\nmeasurable for each $i<n$.\n\n1. If every $g_i$ is nonnegative, then\n   $$\\mathbb E\\left[\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)\\right]=\\prod_{i<n}\\mathbb E[g_i(X_i)]$$\n   in $[0,+\\infty]$. In this finite product of expectations, a zero factor\n   makes the product zero even when another factor is $+\\infty$; otherwise\n   multiplication has its usual extended nonnegative meaning.\n2. If every $g_i(X_i)$ is integrable, then $\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)$ is integrable\n   and the same factorization holds in $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "cor-markov-inequality-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is a nonnegative random variable on a probability\nspace and $a>0$, then\n$$\\mathbb P(X\\ge a)\\le\\frac{\\mathbb E[X]}{a}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F17",
          "source": "def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S,\\Sigma)$ be a random element. Its\n**law** or **distribution** is the set function\n$$\\mathbb P_X:\\Sigma\\to[0,+\\infty],\\qquad \\mathbb P_X(B):=\\mathbb P(X^{-1}(B)).$$\n\nThus the law of $X$ records the probability of each measurable target set by\npulling it back to an event in the original probability space.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F18",
          "source": "thm-change-of-variables-for-expectation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S,\\Sigma)$ be a random element, let\n$\\mathbb P_X$ be its law, and let $g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb R$ or\n$g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable.\n\n1. If $g\\ge0$, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$\n2. If $g(X)$ is integrable, then $g$ is integrable with respect to\n   $\\mathbb P_X$ and the same formula holds:\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F19",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F20",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$, let\n$\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and let $c \\in A$. Suppose $f$ and $g$ are continuous at\n$c$ ([[def-continuity-real]]). Then:\n\n1. $f + g$, $\\alpha f$ and $fg$ are continuous at $c$;\n2. $|f|$, the function $x \\mapsto |f(x)|$, is continuous at $c$;\n3. $\\max\\{f,g\\}$ and $\\min\\{f,g\\}$, defined pointwise by\n   $x \\mapsto \\max\\{f(x), g(x)\\}$ and $x \\mapsto \\min\\{f(x), g(x)\\}$\n   ([[def-max-min]]), are continuous at $c$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is continuous at\n   $c$ as a function on $A_0$.\n\nMoreover, with no hypothesis at all:\n\n5. every constant function $A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and the identity\n   $\\mathrm{id} : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto x$, are continuous on $A$; hence\n   so is $x \\mapsto x^{n}$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n   ([[def-integer-power]]), and hence so is every **polynomial function**\n   $x \\mapsto a_0 + a_1 x + \\dots + a_n x^{n}$ with real coefficients.\n\nConsequently, if $f$ and $g$ are continuous **on** $A$ then so are $f+g$,\n$\\alpha f$, $fg$, $|f|$, $\\max\\{f,g\\}$ and $\\min\\{f,g\\}$, and\n$(f/g)|_{A_0}$ is continuous on $A_0$.\n\n**Claim 4 is stated on $A_0$ because $f/g$ is not defined where $g$ vanishes**,\nand $g$ may well vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$. The hypothesis is\n$g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ nowhere zero\"; what it buys is that $c$ itself lies in\nthe smaller domain, which is what makes continuity there mean anything.\n\n**Nothing here is proved through a sequence.** Claims 1 and 4 are read off from\n[[thm-algebra-of-function-limits]], which is itself proved from $\\varepsilon$\nand $\\delta$, and claims 2, 3 and 5 are proved directly below. So no choice\nprinciple is used anywhere in this item.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F21",
          "source": "thm-composition-of-continuous-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g(A) \\subseteq B$, and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$. If $g$ is continuous\nat $c$ and $f$ is continuous at $g(c) \\in B$, then $f \\circ g$ is continuous at\n$c$ ([[def-continuity-real]]).\n\nConsequently, if $g$ is continuous on $A$ and $f$ is continuous on $B$, then\n$f \\circ g$ is continuous on $A$.\n\n**No side hypothesis is needed, and that is the whole point.** The composition\ntheorem for limits, [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]], must assume one of\ntwo extra conditions: either $L \\in B$ with $f(L) = M$, or $g \\ne L$ on a\npunctured neighbourhood of $c$; with both dropped the statement is false, which\nis [[fs-naive-composition-of-limits]]. The first of those conditions is exactly\n*continuity of $f$ at $L$* written out, so under the hypotheses above it holds\nautomatically and nothing has to be assumed. The mechanism is visible in the\nproof: [[def-continuity-real]] quantifies over $|y - b| < \\rho$ rather than over\n$0 < |y - b| < \\rho$, so the value $y = b$ that the limit version cannot control\nis precisely the one the continuity hypothesis does control.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F22",
          "source": "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
