      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For each sample coordinate $i$, the projections $S\\mapsto(X_i,Y_i)$ and $S\\mapsto X_i$ are measurable by [F8]. The map $T_i:S\\mapsto(S,X_i)$ is measurable because the preimage of a measurable rectangle $B\\times C$ is $B\\cap\\{S:X_i\\in C\\}$ and such rectangles generate the product sigma-algebra [F8]; composition with $F$ is measurable by [F9], so $S\\mapsto f_S(X_i)$ is measurable. For each fixed $a$, $S\\mapsto g_{s,a}(X_i)$ is measurable by [F9]. The selector $\\alpha$ takes values in the finite set $\\mathcal A$ by [F2], so the preimage of a Borel set under $S\\mapsto g_{s,\\alpha(S)}(X_i)$ is a finite union of intersections of measurable selector fibers and fixed-code preimages. By [F10], each coordinatewise distortion comparison is measurable, and the finite intersection over coordinates and sample positions makes the compression-success event measurable. The zero-one loss $\\ell_a(x,y)=\\mathbf1\\{h_{s,a}(x)\\ne y\\}$ is measurable by the verified measurability properties in [F3]; composing it with the sample projections and taking a finite average shows that each $\\widehat R_S(h_{s,a})$ is measurable. Since $\\mathcal A$ is finite and $\\alpha$ measurable, both selected risk maps $S\\mapsto R_P(h_{s,\\alpha(S)})$ and $S\\mapsto\\widehat R_S(h_{s,\\alpha(S)})$ are measurable. The margins $M_{f_S}(X_i,Y_i)$ are measurable by [F10], hence so is their finite threshold average $\\widehat L_{2\\epsilon}(f_S;S)$. Thus every event in the Statement and proof is measurable.",
          "inputs": [
