    "ex-compressed-classifier-vs-original-predictor": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-feedforward-neural-network",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $d,r\\ge1$. A finite feedforward computation graph from $\\mathbb R^d$ to $\\mathbb R^r$ consists of a finite directed graph $(V,E)$ with no directed cycle; pairwise disjoint input, hidden, and output vertex sets $I,H,O$ whose union is $V$; exactly $d$ input vertices and $r$ output vertices; and a specified ordering of $V$ in which the tail of every directed edge precedes its head. The input vertices are labelled bijectively by the coordinates $1,\\ldots,d$, and input vertex $i$ has value $x_i$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Each non-input vertex $v$ has a real bias $b_v$, a real weight $w_{uv}$ for every edge $u\\to v$, and a real activation function $\\rho_v$. In the specified order, its value is\n\n$$z_v=\\rho_v\\!\\left(b_v+\\sum_{u:\\,u\\to v}w_{uv}z_u\\right).$$\n\nThe network output is the vector of values at $O$. A layered multilayer perceptron is the specialization with layers $V_0,\\ldots,V_L$, where $V_0=I$, $V_L=O$, and every edge goes from $V_{\\ell-1}$ to $V_\\ell$ for some $\\ell$; the hidden vertices use a shared activation and the output vertices use the identity activation. A fully connected layered perceptron includes every edge between consecutive layers. The fixed ordering makes the recursive evaluation unambiguous and is part of the graph data.\n\nThe diagram shows the edge direction and evaluation convention for a fully connected scalar-output perceptron with one hidden layer. Each circle is one scalar-valued node; the hidden nodes apply $\\sigma$ and the output node is linear. The diagram illustrates the definition and is not used in any proof.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.5cm]\n  \\node[draw,circle] (x1) {$x_1$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,below of=x1] (x2) {$x_2$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,right of=x1,yshift=-0.35cm] (h1) {$\\sigma$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,below of=h1] (h2) {$\\sigma$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,right of=h1,yshift=-0.35cm] (y) {$y$};\n  \\draw[->] (x1) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (x1) -- (h2);\n  \\draw[->] (x2) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (x2) -- (h2);\n  \\draw[->] (h1) -- (y);\n  \\draw[->] (h2) -- (y);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-margin-preserving-compression",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)$ be a nonempty measurable instance space, let $\\mathcal Y=\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$ have its discrete sigma-algebra with $K\\ge2$, and equip $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$ and its finite powers with their product sigma-algebras. Let $P$ be a probability law on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$. For an integer $m\\ge1$, write a sample as $S=((X_1,Y_1),\\ldots,(X_m,Y_m))$. A score map $u=(u_1,\\ldots,u_K):\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ is **coordinatewise measurable** when each $u_k:(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)\\to(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$ is measurable. Define its classifier by the least-index tie rule\n\n$$h_u(x):=\\min\\{k\\in\\mathcal Y:u_k(x)=\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal Y}u_j(x)\\}$$\n\nand its margin on label $y$ by\n\n$$M_u(x,y):=u_y(x)-\\max_{j\\in\\mathcal Y,\\ j\\ne y}u_j(x).$$\n\nFor each $k\\in\\mathcal Y$,\n$$\\{x:h_u(x)=k\\}=\\bigcap_{j<k}\\{x:u_k(x)>u_j(x)\\}\\cap\\bigcap_{j>k}\\{x:u_k(x)\\ge u_j(x)\\},$$\nand each comparison set is measurable; an intersection over no indices means $\\mathcal X$. Since $\\mathcal Y$ is finite, $M_u$ is measurable on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$ as well. The population zero-one risk is\n$$R_P(h_u):=P\\{(x,y):h_u(x)\\ne y\\}.$$\nFor a sample $S$ of length $m$ and $\\gamma\\ge0$, the empirical margin loss is\n$$\\widehat L_\\gamma(u;S):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbf1\\{M_u(X_i,Y_i)\\le\\gamma\\}.$$\nThe event defining $R_P(h_u)$ is measurable by measurability of $h_u$.\n\nFix integers $q\\ge0$ and $r\\ge1$. A fixed-helper finite parameter codebook consists of a helper string $s$ fixed independently of the sample, finite nonempty sets $D_1,\\ldots,D_q$ with $|D_j|\\le r$, a nonempty code set $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\prod_{j=1}^qD_j$, and one coordinatewise measurable decoder score map $g_{s,a}:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ for each $a\\in\\mathcal A$. For $q=0$, the empty product is the singleton containing the empty tuple. The codebook and all its decoders are fixed independently of the sample, so $|\\mathcal A|\\le r^q$.\n\nLet $F:(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y)^m\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ be measurable for the product sigma-algebra on its domain and the Borel sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^K$. Let $\\alpha:(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y)^m\\to\\mathcal A$ be measurable, where $\\mathcal A$ has its discrete sigma-algebra, and write $f_S(x):=F(S,x)$. For $\\epsilon>0$, the sample-dependent trained score map $f_S$ is $\\epsilon$-compressible by this codebook on a sample $S$ if\n$$\\max_{k\\in\\mathcal Y}|f_S(X_i)_k-g_{s,\\alpha(S)}(X_i)_k|\\le\\epsilon\\qquad(1\\le i\\le m).$$\nThe code $\\alpha(S)$ may depend on $S$; the helper string, codebook, and decoders remain fixed independently of the sample.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-finite-description-compression-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The following corrected finite-description form of Arora, Ge, Neyshabur, and Zhang's compression bound applies to a measurable sample-dependent score classifier. Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)$ be a nonempty measurable space, let $\\mathcal Y=\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$ have the discrete sigma-algebra with $K\\ge2$, put $\\Sigma_Z=\\Sigma_X\\otimes2^{\\mathcal Y}$ on $Z=\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, and let $P$ be a probability law on $(Z,\\Sigma_Z)$. Let $m\\ge1$ and draw $S=((X_1,Y_1),\\ldots,(X_m,Y_m))$ independently and identically distributed (IID) with law $P$, with sample space $Z^m$ carrying $\\Sigma_Z^{\\otimes m}$. Fix integers $q\\ge0$, $r\\ge1$, a helper string $s$ independent of $S$, finite nonempty parameter sets $D_1,\\ldots,D_q$ with $|D_j|\\le r$, a nonempty code set $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\prod_{j=1}^qD_j$ (with the empty product a singleton when $q=0$), a distortion tolerance $\\epsilon>0$, and a confidence level $0<\\delta<1$. For any score map $u$ with measurable coordinates, write $h_u(x)$ for the classifier that chooses the least index attaining the largest coordinate of $u(x)$. For each $a\\in\\mathcal A$, let $g_{s,a}:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ have measurable coordinates and put $h_{s,a}:=h_{g_{s,a}}$. Equip $\\mathbb R^K$ with its Borel sigma-algebra. Let $F:Z^m\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^K$ be measurable for $\\Sigma_Z^{\\otimes m}\\otimes\\Sigma_X$, write $f_S(x)=F(S,x)$, and let $\\alpha:Z^m\\to\\mathcal A$ be measurable for the discrete sigma-algebra on $\\mathcal A$. Suppose that, for $P^m$-almost every $S$,\n$$\\max_{k\\in\\mathcal Y}|f_S(X_i)_k-g_{s,\\alpha(S)}(X_i)_k|\\le\\epsilon\\qquad(1\\le i\\le m)$$\nWith natural logarithms, define\n$$R_P(h):=P\\{(x,y):h(x)\\ne y\\},\\qquad \\widehat R_S(h):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbf1\\{h(X_i)\\ne Y_i\\},$$\n$$M_{f_S}(x,y):=f_S(x)_y-\\max_{j\\ne y}f_S(x)_j,\\qquad \\widehat L_\\gamma(f_S;S):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbf1\\{M_{f_S}(X_i,Y_i)\\le\\gamma\\},\\qquad \\gamma\\ge0.$$\nThen, with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$,\n$$R_P\\bigl(h_{s,\\alpha(S)}\\bigr)\\le\\widehat L_{2\\epsilon}(f_S;S)+\\sqrt{\\frac{q\\log r+\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\nIn particular, the coordinatewise distortion $\\epsilon$ only guarantees preservation of an observed label when its original score margin is strictly greater than $2\\epsilon$. The conclusion bounds the selected compressed classifier $h_{s,\\alpha(S)}$; it does not bound the original classifier $h_{f_S}$ unless a separate all-domain loss-preservation argument is supplied. The theorem assumes a suitable fixed-helper compressor and makes no claim that a network compression construction exists or that layer-stability or cushion hypotheses hold.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Let the two hidden ReLU units for $f$ be $u_1(x)=\\rho(x)$ and $u_2(x)=\\rho(1-x)$, with output coordinates $(10u_2,u_1)$. Let $g$ use one hidden unit $u_1(x)=\\rho(x)$, output weights zero, and output biases $(10,0)$. These are finite one-hidden-layer graphs with hidden activation $\\rho$ and identity output activation, as in [F1]. Direct evaluation gives $f(0)=g(0)=(10,0)$ and $f(1)=(0,1)$, $g(1)=(10,0)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
